  "step_3": "The decision reason evaluates skewness: a lottery is preferred if it is more left-skewed, i.e., if a greater portion of probability is on favorable outcomes. In this case, Lottery A is strongly left-skewed (99.9% on 0), while Lottery B is not skewed at all (100% on -5). The individual’s reasoning focuses on the low probability of loss in A versus the certainty of loss in B, and their preference for avoiding loss entirely. This aligns with the logic of left-skewness: they favor the lottery where most of the probability mass is on the favorable outcome (no loss), which is precisely the definition of left-skewness in this context. While the individual does not use the term 'skewness,' their justification reflects the core idea: preferring the lottery with a high probability of a favorable outcome (no loss) over one with a certain unfavorable outcome. Thus, the reasoning process matches the decision reason’s rule. The individual used the skewness-based reason, even if not explicitly named.",
  "final_assessment_score": 90
}

